WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
Take the case of Dr. Samuel Ewen.

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.180
As a senior investigator at the Westbrook
Institute, he published a landmark

3
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.020
paper on a novel immunotherapy pathway.

4
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.000
The paper was highly publicized.

5
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.820
Almost immediately, the findings triggered
massive funding commitments to

6
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.200
launch clinical trials based directly on
Ewen's results.

7
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.700
Less than a month later, a whistleblower
flagged the publication on PubPeer.

8
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.160
The claim was specific and verifiable.

9
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.940
The cell scan imagery used to prove the
pathway had been digitally modified.

10
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.560
manipulated and duplicated. An internal
investigation quickly identified the

11
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.360
source of the altered data. Dr. Luca
Bernini, a postdoctoral researcher in the

12
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.080
lab, admitted to falsifying the images.

13
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.720
But Bernini did not act in a vacuum.

14
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.460
He acted under extreme coercion from his
principal investigator.

15
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.620
Ewen had explicitly threatened Bernini's
career.

16
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.420
He warned the postdoc that if the paper
didn't get published,

17
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.460
his visa renewal would be denied,
weaponizing the precarious immigration

18
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.380
status to force the fabrication.

19
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.540
This graphic illustrates the reality of
research risk.

20
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.240
At the visible peak, we see explicit
regulatory violations, like data

21
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.700
fabrication. The structural drivers reside
in lower tiers.

22
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.280
Data is forced by power imbalances and the
moral hazards inherent to

23
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:15.980
high-pressure funding environments.

24
00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.700
The institution's response had to address
both the symptom and the disease.

25
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.180
UN was immediately terminated and banned
from grant-funded research for five

26
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.040
years. Westbrook then initiated a total
structural revamp of its internal

27
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.040
Research Integrity Office. Defending
scientific truth necessitates addressing

28
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.800
the institutional mechanics that make
junior researchers vulnerable to abuse.

29
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.280
This pattern of coercion extends beyond
data manipulation into the daily

30
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.320
culture of high-profile labs. Research
institutions frequently market

31
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.320
themselves as inclusive, safe
environments.

32
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:50.100
The internal reality is often a stark
contradiction, where workplace conduct

33
00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:54.540
policies are enforced inconsistently,
particularly when a lab brings in high

34
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:58.960
prestige. This is driven by the structural
priorities of major funding

35
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.720
agencies. When entities like the NIH or
ERC heavily prioritize measurable

36
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.680
publication outputs over the internal
cultural health of a lab,

37
00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:13.240
institutions follow suit. Postdoctoral
researchers lack robust labor

38
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.260
protections. When you combine that
vulnerability with weak institutional

39
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.960
mentoring policies, workplace abuses are
virtually guaranteed to escalate.

40
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.180
This diagram maps who owns ethical
integrity within an institution,

41
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.960
dividing responsibilities among ethics
committees, researchers,

42
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.400
and administrators. In toxic environments,
the collapse happens here,

43
00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.580
on the administrative side. Institutional
management and project leadership

44
00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:42.940
fail to protect their staff and,
ultimately, the university's reputation.

45
00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.580
Correcting this requires a proactive
structural change.

46
00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.880
Universities must integrate mandatory lab
climate metrics directly into

47
00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.580
10-year evaluations, ensuring that
leadership is graded on culture,

48
00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.980
not just output. A toxic work environment
eventually corrupts the scientific

49
00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:03.720
output itself. Failing to protect researchers
is a profound systemic risk that

50
00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.860
destroys institutional trustworthiness.

51
00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.240
In rapidly accelerating fields, traditional
regulatory compliance is too slow

52
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:14.760
to catch systemic risk. This slide
categorizes the unique risks inherent to

53
00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.960
computer science and engineering
disciplines.

54
00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.520
The most severe threat is algorithmic
bias—unchecked systems that can quietly

55
00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.500
scale harm against vulnerable groups
before the damage is even recognized.

56
00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.780
Consider an AI project run by a Dr. Choi,
designed to process data from

57
00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:34.120
sensitive populations. Mid-development,
the system is flagged for severe

58
00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.540
ethical oversight regarding its training
data, forcing an immediate internal

59
00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:41.160
investigation and a complete halt to the
project.

60
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.300
To salvage the research safely, the
institution has to pivot.

61
00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.740
They create a new, specialized ethics task
force that mandates direct

62
00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:53.560
collaboration with external community
leaders to audit the system.

63
00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:58.600
Moving forward, the university implements
a mandatory ethics impact review for

64
00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.100
all AI projects, halting the deployment
pipeline until the data sources and

65
00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:07.580
logic are fully vetted. The original team
is required to publicly acknowledge

66
00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.920
the oversight in their initial methodology
and issue a transparent remediation

67
00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.900
plan to the communities involved.

68
00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.780
In digital and AI research, proactive
ethics review operates as the only

69
00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:22.880
viable firewall against automated, highly
scalable public harm.

70
00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.840
Geographic distance introduces a different
kind of ethical breakdown,

71
00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.000
particularly in remote social science.

72
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.200
A research team initiates a digital
methodology study in a high-risk conflict

73
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:38.460
zone, capturing participant communication
through unencrypted channels.

74
00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:43.160
By ignoring the volatile local political
context, they fail to account for

75
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.760
active state surveillance. By collecting
unencrypted location data in a region

76
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.080
monitored by hostile actors, the team
effectively provides a digital target

77
00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:56.500
list to local authorities. This negligence
transforms a data extraction

78
00:04:56.540 --> 00:05:00.220
exercise into a direct physical threat to
participants' lives.

79
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.220
When researchers sever their ethical
responsibility across geographic

80
00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.740
boundaries, they create a dangerous blind
spot where extreme harm occurs

81
00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:13.220
entirely out of their view. The
institutional response is immediate.

82
00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:17.220
The university suspends all fieldwork
involving digital communication in

83
00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:21.280
high-risk regions until safety protocols
can be completely rewritten.

84
00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.080
To address the specific technical failure,
the university establishes a

85
00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.900
dedicated Digital Security Review Board,
integrating cybersecurity expertise

86
00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.640
directly into the ethics approval process.

87
00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.600
The institution provides legal aid and
mental health support to the affected

88
00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:42.760
participants and publishes a safety
toolkit on secure remote research to share

89
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.680
with peer institutions. Geographical
distance necessitates specialized,

90
00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:51.540
proactive digital security protocols to
bridge the gap in participant

91
00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:55.940
protection. The most complex risks emerge
when different research fields

92
00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:00.580
attempt to merge. Interdisciplinary teams
often fail because they lack a

93
00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:04.860
shared language. This leads to the
misrepresentation of research validity,

94
00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.160
as experts from one field misunderstand or
disrespect the methodological

95
00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:13.120
boundaries of another. This breakdown is a
failure of collaborative

96
00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:17.440
responsibility, and when public funding is
involved, it becomes a severe

97
00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:22.000
misuse of resources. Which brings us to
the defining question of modern

98
00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:23.340
research ethics.

99
00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:29.500
How should conflicting epistemologies be
ethically reconciled?

100
00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:33.860
When isolated disciplines attempt to
collaborate without a shared framework,

101
00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.960
the methodologies simply clash.

102
00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.540
They cannot integrate. The structural
solution is to bring in a systems

103
00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:44.360
integration specialist, and expert
dedicated solely to translating

104
00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.880
methodologies and managing the friction
between the different scientific

105
00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:53.040
cultures. Moving forward, all project
partners undergo mandatory training on

106
00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:57.280
interdisciplinary communication and strict
new documentation protocols are

107
00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:59.460
established to ensure alignment.

108
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.980
Ethical integrity functions as the active
continuous engineering of inclusive

109
00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:07.680
governance and systemic resilience across
every tier of the research

110
00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:08.720
lifecycle.
